1. OVERVIEW AND SYSTEM ACCESSIBILITY
LibNet (SYS-LIB) is the Integrated Library Management System and Online Public Access Catalog (OPAC) of Demo University. It manages book cataloging, circulation status, fine ledgers, electronic resource links, and institutional repository access.
- Student & Public OPAC URL: https://libnet.demo.edu.internal/opac
- Library Staff Administrative URL: https://libnet.demo.edu.internal/staff

2. CATALOG SEARCH MODES AND STACK DISCOVERY
2.1 Basic Catalog Search: Users can query the repository by entering keywords, book titles, author surnames, or ISBNs in the primary LibNet search bar.
2.2 Advanced Filtering Options: Search results can be filtered by publication year, department collection (e.g., Computer Science, Electrical, Mechanical, Management), language, and resource type (Print Book, E-Book, Bound Periodical, Dissertation).
2.3 Interpreting Stack Availability and Shelf Locations:
- Status 'Available': Indicates the volume is present on the shelf; the display shows the Floor Number, Stacks Aisle, and Dewey Decimal Call Number (e.g., Floor 2, Aisle 4B, Call No: 005.133 COR);
- Status 'Issued': Displays current loan status and projected due date of return;
- Status 'Reference Only': Restricted strictly to in-house reading room consultation;
- Status 'On Reserve': Held temporarily at the circulation reserve counter for specific academic courses.

3. BOOK RESERVATION AND HOLD PLACEMENT (PROC-LIB-CIRCULATION)
When a required book is currently issued to another borrower, registered patrons can place an automated reservation hold:
3.1 Hold Placement: Click 'Place Hold' on the catalog entry and authenticate using student ERP credentials;
3.2 Queue Prioritization: Holds are queued strictly on a first-come, first-served basis;
3.3 Return Alert and 48-Hour Collection Window: Upon physical return of the reserved book, LibNet dispatches an automated email/SMS alert to the reserving patron and places the volume in the 'Hold Shelf' at the Circulation Desk. The book is reserved exclusively for forty-eight (48) hours; if not collected within 48 hours, the hold expires and transfers to the next user in queue or returns to open circulation.

4. ONLINE BOOK RENEWAL WORKFLOW
Patrons may renew borrowed circulating items from any personal device without carrying books back to the library counter:
4.1 Step 1: Log in to https://libnet.demo.edu.internal/opac using institutional Single Sign-On (SSO);
4.2 Step 2: Navigate to 'My Account' > 'Current Checkouts';
4.3 Step 3: Identify the book to extend and click 'Renew Selected';
4.4 Conditions for Renewal Approval:
- The renewal request must be submitted prior to the original due date;
- No other patron has placed an active reservation hold on the item;
- The patron's cumulative unpaid fine balance in LibNet does not exceed Rs. 50;
- The book has not exceeded the maximum limit of two (2) consecutive online renewals.

5. RFID SELF-CHECKOUT KIOSK OPERATING INSTRUCTIONS
Patrons can borrow and return materials autonomously using self-service kiosks located in the Ground Floor Circulation Foyer:
5.1 Tap Student ID: Place your Demo University Smart RFID ID card against the contactless reader on the kiosk console;
5.2 Place Books on Reader Pad: Position up to four (4) books simultaneously on the RFID sensor surface;
5.3 Verify Screen Display: Ensure all book titles, barcodes, and computed due dates appear correctly on the touchscreen display;
5.4 Complete Checkout: Select 'Confirm Borrowing' and choose to print a paper receipt or send a digital receipt to your student email; the RFID security gate sensor is automatically deactivated for thirty (30) seconds to enable exit without alarm triggers.

1. STATUTORY CONSTITUTION AND MANDATE
The Central Library Advisory Committee (frequently designated as the Library Committee or 'LC') is a statutory academic governance body constituted under the authority of the Vice-Chancellor and Academic Council (GOV-REG-002). The committee advises the University Chief Librarian on institutional information policies, collection development strategies, electronic resource subscriptions, annual acquisition budget allocations, and physical facility development.

2. CONTROLLED POLYSEMY DISAMBIGUATION (LC)
Within university administrative terminology, the abbreviation 'LC' signifies two distinct institutional constructs:
2.1 Library Committee (LC under DEPT-LIB / LIB-GOV-001): The statutory advisory and supervisory body determining book acquisition quotas, journal database subscriptions, fine rates, and library facility rules;
2.2 Leaving Certificate (LC under DEPT-ADM / ADM-POL-004): The official institutional discharge document and bonafide release certificate issued by the Admissions Office and Registrar upon graduation or program withdrawal.
- Query Disambiguation Rule: Inquiries regarding discharge, migration, leaving college, or bonafide study termination refer to the Leaving Certificate (ADM-POL-004); inquiries concerning books, journal subscriptions, reading room expansions, or library governance refer to the Library Committee (LIB-GOV-001).

3. COMMITTEE COMPOSITION AND MEMBERSHIP
The Library Advisory Committee comprises ten (10) members appointed for a two-year tenure:
3.1 Chairperson: A senior full Professor appointed by the Vice-Chancellor upon recommendation of the Dean of Academic Affairs;
3.2 Member Secretary: The University Chief Librarian (ROLE-LIB), serving as ex-officio executive convener;
3.3 Faculty Representatives: One tenured faculty member nominated from each Academic School:
- School of Computing and Data Sciences (SCH-SCDS);
- School of Electrical and Electronics Engineering (SCH-SEEE);
- School of Mechanical and Infrastructure Engineering (SCH-SMIE);
- School of Management and Commerce (SCH-SMC);
3.4 Administrative Ex-Officio Members:
- Director of IT Infrastructure (DEPT-IT) to coordinate enterprise bandwidth and LibNet integration;
- Chief Finance Officer / Bursar (DEPT-FIN) to oversee fiscal budget execution;
3.5 Student Representatives: One nominated doctoral research scholar and one postgraduate student representative selected on academic merit to communicate patron requirements.

4. POWERS, AUTHORITIES, AND ADVISORY FUNCTIONS
The Library Committee is empowered to:
4.1 Electronic Database and Journal Allocations: Review and approve annual renewal proposals for high-value electronic research subscriptions (IEEE Xplore, ScienceDirect, ACM Digital Library, Scopus) based on usage metrics provided by LibNet;
4.2 Departmental Book Procurement Grants: Formulate formulas for equitable distribution of book procurement funds across engineering and management departments;
4.3 Circulation Policies and Fine Schedules: Formulate recommendations for modifying borrowing quotas, overdue fine rates, and account lockout thresholds under POL-LIBRARY;
4.4 Infrastructure Expansion: Approve operational enhancements, including the allocation of 24/7 reading rooms, creation of digital discussion cubicles, and installation of automated RFID kiosks.

5. MEETING CONVENING, QUORUM, AND RESOLUTIONS
5.1 Meeting Frequency: The committee convenes at least once per academic semester (Ordinary Meetings in September and February). Extraordinary meetings may be convened by the Chairperson or Member Secretary to address urgent procurement deadlines.
5.2 Quorum: A minimum of six (6) members, including the Chairperson, Member Secretary, and at least two Academic School faculty nominees, constitutes valid quorum.
5.3 Minutes and Ratification: Committee proceedings and resolutions are drafted by the Member Secretary, circulated to members within ten (10) working days, and submitted to the Academic Council and Vice-Chancellor for statutory ratification.
